 take them  off but we also have to air the  classroom every 20 minutes  at least three to five minutes  airing the rooms another measure that  hasn't changed since last year  additional air filters or purification  systems have not been installed  open windows and doors ought to be  enough  we have no problem whatsoever with the  ventilation the children have their  jackets with them  they can take them on and off again  that's totally fine  the parent's representative disagrees he  wants air filters  especially for elementary schools we  called for purification systems 12  months ago precisely to protect the  weakest of society  those who cannot be vaccinated children  under the age of 12.  unfortunately that didn't happen we're  exactly at the same level as last year  and that's not good  in germany covet infections are rising  again  yet everybody hopes that students will  be able to come to school for as long as  possible let's talk more about this with  david evans he's a senior fellow at the  center for  global development working on education  health  and social safety nets welcome to dw  david  distance learning has surged during the  pandemic  what does a successful concept for  distance learning look like  on the whole distance learning during  the pandemic has not been a great  success but if we think about successful  distance learning we have to think about  what  good teaching and learning look like in  general students need interaction with  skilled and motivated teachers that's  what they need when they're  in school in person and if we want any  positive results from distance learning  we have to replicate those principles  even in high income countries for the  first months of shutdown most education  was  totally asynchronous teachers would send  some materials home and students would  do the work  over time these high income environments  were able to move to  distance learning which was which much  better replicated  good teaching uh during a non-pandemic  where  teachers were actually interacting with  students  so technology seems to be playing  a an important role here uh for  students and teachers to communicate  with each other despite not being  in the same room so one would assume  that  countries that can provide this  technology advanced countries  can also provide the best distance  learning concept is that correct  so this is absolutely true in high  income countries what you've seen is  classes using things like zoom and  google classroom  where teachers are able to interact with  students in most low-income countries  what this distance learning has looked  like  has been much more like maybe a radio  program or a television program once a  day  and if you can imagine trying to learn  calculus or physics from a radio show  it's just very difficult to  to imagine now there have been some  exceptions so in a middle-income country  like botswana  teachers were called upon to call  students  primary school students to help them  work through mathematics problems  and we saw significant learning gains  amongst those students relative to  students who  who didn't have access to that so there  are alternatives in places where there  isn't strong  internet and zoom setups but it is a  huge  challenge so we see all the best efforts  from all sides here  in order to create a learning  environment for students  but still there are studies that show  remote students are more stressed than  their peers in the classroom  why is that so  we see these students they don't have  the opportunity to  interact with other students they don't  have the opportunities to relax between  classes and beyond that what we've seen  even in high income countries as you say  with the best efforts  what we see is over the course of the  last year with distance learning  the very the highest income students in  high income countries haven't seen much  learning loss relative to other years  but lower income students in even in  high income countries where  there's all this technology available  haven't had  uh haven't had the same learning gains  we've seen significant learning loss  the reason for that is that those  students well normally they be in school  and a teacher could help  supervise and their peers could help  encourage those students are sitting at  home  maybe staring at a laptop and the  students who have  fewer resources often also have parents  who  don't have the flexibility to work from  home and to provide them supervision and  support  to keep them focused on school during  the school day  now speaking of learning loss children  are missing  school in latin america for example 160  days almost on average during the first  year of the pandemic  what impact is this having on a  generation of students  the impact is enormous so in some  countries the amount of schooling that  students lost  have lost has amounts to a large part of  the schooling that they'd be expected to  receive  over the course of their entire lifetime  in chad  sudan south sudan children have lost  more than 10 percent of  all the education that they'll be  expected to have  over their entire lives now this  translates into not only a loss of  learning  but a loss of incomes and a loss of  opportunities  for individuals and families and in  terms of economic growth looking forward  and how are societies going to be able  to offset this impact  so there are some countries that have  started to invest  in bringing children back up to speed so  we have proven strategies  of targeting children who've fallen  behind especially these lower income  students who have had fewer resources  during the pandemic  so the uk has is rolling out a  large-scale tutoring program  we have evidence from countries like  donna and india  from the past from before the pandemic  that this kind of targeted tutoring  either with tutors after school or with  teaching assistants during the school  day  can really bring students 